messages:
maria: Noticing rising onboarding tickets: folks confused about how to import the JS SDK across environments. Quickstart currently shows one pattern and it breaks in some setups.
sam: Yup. Common combos we see: Node CJS, Node ESM, TS without esModuleInterop, and Edge (which requires ESM). People paste the quickstart and hit "named export not found" or get `undefined` when using require.
tyler: I think we should ship a small cheatsheet in the README + 4 tiny example apps. Copy/paste is what matters for first-time users.

anita: Agree. Proposed README snippets (short, pragmatic):
```
// Node CommonJS (package.json without "type": "module")
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood.InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Node ESM ("type": "module" or .mjs)
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood.InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Preferred ESM named import (works in strict ESM builds)
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client2 = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// TypeScript (if you don't enable esModuleInterop)
import Redwood = require('@redwood/inference'); // old-style TS import
const clientTs = new (Redwood as any).InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
```

sam: Add a short note about when each pattern is likely to fail:
- require() may return a namespace object with a .default property when package is published with ESM default export only.
- import { Named } fails if the package only exposes a default.
- TS users without esModuleInterop will need either the allowSyntheticDefaultImports flag or the `import = require()` workaround.

maria: Can we also add a tiny tsconfig snippet to the docs to reduce friction? People keep pasting .ts examples that error for them.
anita: Yep, I'll add this suggestion in the docs:```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true
  }
}
```

tyler: Operational action items: 1) docs PR with cheatsheet + 4 example repos (cjs/esm/ts/edge); 2) eng-build ticket to confirm publish outputs include both CJS and ESM and `.d.ts` types that point to the correct export layout.
sam: Filed INF-342 to normalize build outputs and CI tests. Also adding an E2E check in examples CI that runs each sample in node and a simple edge runner (vercel dev).
docs-bot: Draft PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/512 0 reviewers. Includes README section and links to example apps.
maria: For the immediate fix let's update the quickstart to include both CJS + ESM snippets and a one-liner: "If you see \"named export not found\" try the default import or enable esModuleInterop in TS."
tyler: One more nit: ensure package.json includes both "main" (CJS) and "module" (ESM) entries and that our published tarball contains both dist/cjs and dist/esm builds plus types entry in package.json. If we can't change publish this week, docs + examples is the fastest help.
anita: I'll prepare example app scaffolds under examples/js/{cjs,esm,ts,edge} and link to them from quickstart. Will also add minimal README in each showing exact node/npm commands to run.
sam: After docs land, update onboarding checklist and support playbook with the import patterns and INF-342 reference.
maria: Thanks everyone. Please ping when PR is ready and I'll ship a micro-update to onboarding docs. Closing this conversation.
